  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Caribbean Business Enabling Environment Reform (CBEE-r)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Barbados and Eastern Caribbean):  The CBEE-R activity will support ESC governments and private sector stakeholders to strengthen legal and regulatory structures in specific areas, build institutional capacity to properly implement business reforms, and facilitate public-private dialogue to define and support reform priorities and initiatives. An assessment of the unique situation in each country will be done prior to implementation of the activity. It will be implemented in two phases. Phase one will include Guyana, Suriname, Saint Lucia, and Grenada, while phase two will focus on Barbados, St. Vincent and the Grenadines, Dominica, and St. Kitts and Nevis...Significantly constrained economic growth across the ESC has created the circumstances for unsustainable high public borrowing. There is a need for businesses and governments in the region to undertake reforms that will catalyze economic growth while simultaneously building resilience to disasters and improving risk mitigation in order to create sustainable, strong Caribbean economies...CBEE-R will improve services under five components: Electricity, Connectivity and Digital Economy, Digital Financial Services, Access to Finance, and Informality. Each of the sectors will be strengthened by introducing approaches to eliminate or reduce current bottlenecks or require upgraded systems. Under a sixth focus area, the activity will build the capacity of key stakeholders to support the development of a strong mechanism for public-private dialogue. By combining all approaches, CBEE-R will improve the regional environment for businesses to develop and thrive, promoting economic growth for each of the ESC countries involved and the overall Caribbean region. This activity will support USAID/ESC Mission&apos;s objective to promote a safe, prosperous, and resilient Caribbean region that promotes accountable institutions, economic development, and private sector-led growth, all in support of the Agency&apos;s Private Sector Engagement</narrative>
  </description>
